 | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/366415/brenti-carnevale-unimodality-conjecture-for-residue-class-in)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; # Brenti–Carnevale unimodality conjecture for residue-class inversion polynomials ... Nathan Chapelier-Laget and Jean Fromentin, “A counterexample to a Brenti-Carnevale conjecture”, arXiv:2412.19593 (2024).\n\n# Brenti–Carnevale unimodality conjecture for residue-class inversion polynomials\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nLet $W(A_n)$W(A n​) be the Weyl group of type $A_n$A n​, let $\\mathcal{I}(w)$I(w) be the inversion set of $w$w, and for $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$k∈N and a residue $h$h modulo $k$k define\n\n$$\\mathcal{I}_{k,h}(w):=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{I}(w)\\mid j-i=h\\ \\operatorname{mod} k\\},$$I k,h​(w):={(i,j)∈I(w)∣j−i=h mod k}, $$\\mathfrak{inv}_{k,h}(w):=|\\mathcal{I}_{k,h}(w)|.$$inv k,h​(w):=∣I k,h​(w)∣.\n\nSet\n\n$$B_{n,k,1}(q):=\\sum_{w\\in W(A_n)}q^{\\mathfrak{inv}_{k,1}(w)}.$$B n,k,1​(q):=w∈W(A n​)∑​q inv k,1​(w).\n\nBrenti–Carnevale's conjecture. For any $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$n∈N and any $k\\geq 3$k≥3, the polynomial $B_{n,k,1}(q)$B n,k,1​(q) is unimodal.\n\nThis generalizes the odd-length generating polynomial by selecting inversions in a prescribed residue class of their heights. The source does not specify a resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nNathan Chapelier-Laget and Jean Fromentin, “A counterexample to a Brenti-Carnevale conjecture”, arXiv:2412.19593 (2024).\n\ncoxeter-groupsenumerative-combinatorics\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#14019 - Dyck Words, Pattern Avoidance, and Automatic Sequences (https://cm.episciences.org/14